{"chunk_id": "16f282ee835d", "text": "Shurpanakha fiercely rebukes Ravana for his negligence as king, arguing that a ruler who ignores intelligence, policy, and danger will lose his kingdom. She reveals that Rama has already slain fourteen thousand rakshasas, including Khara and Dushana, single\u2011handedly in the Dandaka forest, restoring safety to the sages. Provoked and curious, Ravana demands details about this warrior who mutilated his sister. In a furious yet admiring account, Shurpanakha describes Rama\u2019s beauty, martial power, and storm of deadly arrows, along with the loyalty of his brother Lakshmana. She ends by portraying Rama\u2019s wife Sita as extraordinarily beautiful and devoted. Her speech plants the crucial seed of Ravana\u2019s obsession with Sita, setting in motion the abduction that will drive the central conflict of the Ramayana.\nShurpanakha rebukes Ravana for negligence, describes Rama\u2019s power and Sita\u2019s beauty, and provokes him toward confronting Rama and desiring Sita.\nThemes: provocation, kingship and duty, description of Rama, vengeance, desire for Sita", "vector": [-0.0027873723302036524, -0.023877430707216263, -0.009056033566594124, 0.00519685260951519, 0.00503967609256506, 0.0043708402663469315, -0.01768401265144348, -0.008407263085246086, -0.02498769760131836, -0.02521510049700737, 0.028733177110552788, 0.021041566506028175, 0.014420094899833202, 0.017978299409151077, -0.008219989016652107, 0.01046058814972639, 0.05425594002008438, -0.019021684303879738, 0.017804402858018875, -0.027422258630394936, -0.01705530658364296, -0.01618582010269165, -0.007303684484213591, 0.014687629416584969, -0.02197793684899807, 0.014687629416584969, 0.021095074713230133, -0.03871220350265503, -0.00311677367426455, -0.020225588232278824, -0.034806203097105026, -0.022044820711016655, 0.014674252830445766, -0.007029461674392223, -0.021282348781824112, 0.009276749566197395, 0.004013013560324907, -0.02375704050064087, 0.001205576118081808, 0.00